Full extreme-detail breakdown of the Deep Analysis report dashboard
This image shows a complete Deep Analysis report page for Field Shift, designed as a long, vertically scrolling agricultural intelligence report. It is not a conventional dashboard made up of unrelated statistics cards. It is a structured farm diagnostic report that brings together the farm's overall condition, individual field assessments, risks, strengths, water data, soil information, weather exposure, vegetation, crop rotations, recommendations, historical changes, data quality, methodology, and analysis audit.

The entire interface uses a dark agricultural visual identity: deep forest-green backgrounds, translucent green-black panels, thin green borders, bright emerald indicators, cyan-blue data accents, and amber/red warning indicators.

The screenshot is approximately 694 × 1478 pixels and has a narrow desktop-style layout. It contains a main report column on the left and a persistent-looking utility sidebar on the right. The report continues almost to the bottom of the screenshot.

1. Overall page structure










The page is divided into four major visual areas:
1. Top report control bar – assessment period, last run, report status, export, print, and run-new-analysis actions.
2. Main report content – the actual analysis, beginning with summary metrics and continuing through numbered diagnostic sections.
3. Right-hand utility sidebar – key insights, report actions, quick links, and a promotional agricultural visual.
4. Bottom report information panels – run summary and audit, field evidence timeline, and seasonal review.
The main report content occupies approximately 83–85% of the available width, while the right sidebar occupies approximately 15–17%.

The layout is dense in information but uses consistent spacing, alignment, borders, and section grouping to prevent the report from feeling like one enormous block of text.
Background and overall appearance
- The page background is an almost-black forest green.
- Main cards use a slightly lighter dark green.
- Cards have subtle translucent surfaces.
- Borders are thin, muted green lines.
- Corners are rounded, generally around 10–14px.
- Shadows are subtle and dark.
- Bright green is used for positive conditions and completion states.
- Cyan-blue is used for data-oriented indicators.
- Yellow and amber communicate caution.
- Red is reserved for priority risks and warnings.
- White and pale green are used for primary text.
- Muted gray-green is used for secondary text and metadata.
The background has a faint green atmospheric glow, particularly toward the lower-right side.

The interface should feel like a premium agricultural monitoring and intelligence platform, not a spreadsheet or a conventional admin dashboard.

