15. Important implementation details that should not be lost
There are several important distinctions behind the visual design.

1. Farm condition is not the same as evidence coverage.

A farm can be in good condition while having incomplete data. Similarly, high evidence coverage does not automatically mean the farm is healthy.

2. Confidence is not the same as evidence coverage.

Confidence should describe the strength of the conclusions supported by the available evidence.

3. The field-level assessment must remain visible.

A positive whole-farm summary must not conceal a serious problem affecting one particular field.

4. Missing data must be represented honestly.

If soil, rainfall, irrigation, or satellite data are unavailable, the interface must not fabricate a value. It should display an explicit unavailable or incomplete state.

5. Historical comparisons need compatible reports.

The What Changed section should compare equivalent periods, fields, metrics, and inputs wherever possible. If reports are not comparable, the interface should explain why.

6. Recommendations need supporting evidence.

The farmer should be able to understand why an action was suggested and which field it applies to.

7. The report should preserve its original snapshot.

A report should represent the data and farm information used when it was generated. Later changes to the farm profile should not silently rewrite old reports.

8. Partial reports must remain visibly partial.

The amber PARTIAL REPORT badge is important. It should not be replaced with a green completed state just because some analysis sections succeeded.

